grid contains several plus shapes made of colour 3. Most have a coloured centre, such as 5, 6, 2, 8, 4 or 1. Some are hollow, with a centre of 0.\n\n**What the outputs show**\n- **Coloured lines.** Every plus with a coloured centre sends rays in all four directions, in its centre's colour, along its centre row and centre column.\n- **Rays stop at other pluses.** A ray moves outward from the plus's own arm across background. It stops at the first non-background input cell that belongs to a different plus.\n  - Pair 2: the 6-ray going left along row 3 stops at the arm of the 5-plus, so cells (3,1) and (3,0) stay 0.\n  - Pair 1: the 5-ray going down column 13 stops at the arm of the 4-plus at row 15, so rows 16–17 of that column stay 0.\n- **Vertical lines win over horizontal lines.** Where a vertical ray crosses a horizontal ray, the output always shows the vertical colour.\n  - Pair 1: column 7 stays 6 across rows 7 and 15.\n  - Pair 2: column 5 shows 8 at rows 2 and 3.\n- **A hollow plus behaves like a plus whose colour is 0.** Its vertical \"0-line\" sits on top of the horizontals, which is why every horizontal line has a gap in the hollow plus's centre column.\n  - Pair 1: x=3 is blank in rows 7, 11 and 15.\n  - Pair 3: x=8 is blank in row 3.\n  - Pair 4: x=13 is blank in rows 3, 4 and 21.\n  \n  Its horizontal 0-line changes nothing, because vertical lines override it anyway. For example, in pair 3 the 2-column passes through row 9 unbroken.\n\n**Construction**\n1. Find every plus centre: a cell whose four orthogonal neighbours are all 3. Its colour is whatever sits in the centre cell, which may be 0.\n2. Draw all horizontal rays. Each starts just past the plus's own arm and paints only cells that are 0 in the input. It stops at any non-zero input cell or at the grid edge.\n3. Draw all vertical rays the same way, painting over the horizontal lines.\n\n**Test input**\n- Coloured pluses:\n  - 8 at (3,6)\n  - 4 at (4,13)\n  - 1 at (12,14)\n  - 2 at (14,3)\n- A hollow plus sits at (10,8), so column 8 will cut every horizontal line.\n- The 4-column going down will stop at the 1-plus's left arm at row 12.\n- The 1-column going up will stop at the 4-plus's right arm at row 4.\n- This mirrors exactly what happens with the 5- and 4-pluses in pair 1.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    out = g.copy()\n    emit(out.copy(), \"Input: plus shapes of 3; coloured centres emit lines, hollow centres emit 'blank' lines.\")\n    centres = []\n    for y in range(1, H - 1):\n        for x in range(1, W - 1):\n            if g[y, x] != 3 and all(g[y + dy, x + dx] == 3 for dy, dx in ((1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1))):\n                centres.append((y, x, int(g[y, x])))\n\n    def ray(y, x, dy, dx, c):\n        y, x = y + 2 * dy, x + 2 * dx\n        while 0 <= y < H and 0 <= x < W and g[y, x] == 0:\n            out[y, x] = c\n            y += dy\n            x += dx\n\n    for y, x, c in centres:\n        ray(y, x, 0, 1, c)\n        ray(y, x, 0, -1, c)\n    emit(out.copy(), \"Horizontal rays from each centre in its colour, stopping at other pluses.\")\n    for y, x, c in centres:\n        ray(y, x, 1, 0, c)\n        ray(y, x, -1, 0, c)\n    emit(out.copy(), \"Vertical rays drawn on top (hollow plus clears its column), stopping at other pluses.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"rigid":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- This mirrors exactly what happens with the 5- and 4-pluses in pair 1."}],"rays":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Coloured lines.** Every plus with a coloured centre sends rays in all four directions, in its centre's colour, along its centre row and centre column."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Rays stop at other pluses.** A ray moves outward from the plus's own arm across background."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Pair 2: the 6-ray going left along row 3 stops at the arm of the 5-plus, so cells (3,1) and (3,0) stay 0."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **A hollow plus behaves like a plus whose colour is 0.** Its vertical \"0-line\" sits on top of the horizontals, which is why every horizontal line has a gap in the hollow plus's centre column."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Vertical rays drawn on top (hollow plus clears its column), stopping at other pluses."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"8e9f7141898db058914bed1bf5849ad068586971f3f37e9770c636a7b0710c89","task_sha256":"6e0c91d1b29ea296ece45e9c60ff92b90b147ae14f03ec4fa7f23b2f8ab2f3cc","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
